SimpleHelp's remote-support server accepts OpenID Connect identity tokens without verifying their signature, so a remote, unauthenticated attacker can obtain a technician session on a server that uses an OIDC Authentication Service with a technician group allowed to log in through it; SimpleHelp says servers without such a group cannot be exploited and that group-authenticated logins are disabled by default for new groups ([SimpleHelp, 2026-05-26](https://simple-help.com/security/simplehelp-security-update-2026-05)). Versions 5.5.15 and earlier and some 6.0 pre-releases are affected, and 5.5.16 and 6.0 RC2 fix the flaw ([SimpleHelp, 2026-05-26](https://simple-help.com/security/simplehelp-security-update-2026-05)). Horizon3.ai, which found it, says the attacker creates a new technician and that MFA the SimpleHelp server enforces does not stop it, because technicians register their own MFA method at first login ([Horizon3.ai, 2026-06-12](https://horizon3.ai/attack-research/disclosures/cve-2026-48558-simplehelp-authentication-bypass-iocs/)). CISA added the flaw to its Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalog on 2026-06-29 ([CISA KEV catalog, 2026-06-29](https://www.cisa.gov/sites/default/files/feeds/known_exploited_vulnerabilities.json)). Blackpoint Cyber observed a technician session on an internet-facing server followed by TaskWeaver, an obfuscated Node.js loader delivered as `jquery.js` and run through `node.exe`, which deployed the Djinn infostealer ([Blackpoint Cyber, 2026-06-29](https://blackpointcyber.com/blog/a-djinn-in-the-machine-taskweavers-node-js-intrusion-chain/); [BleepingComputer, 2026-06-29](https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/hackers-exploit-critical-simplehelp-flaw-deploy-new-djinn-infostealer-taskweaver-malware/)).

**Exposure:** SimpleHelp servers on 5.5.15 or earlier (or a 6.0 pre-release before RC2) with an OIDC Authentication Service and a technician group that allows group-authenticated logins ([SimpleHelp, 2026-05-26](https://simple-help.com/security/simplehelp-security-update-2026-05)).

**Detection:** in the SimpleHelp administration console, technicians created through group authentication with names or email addresses nobody recognises, which Horizon3.ai gives as the compromise check alongside the server log ([Horizon3.ai, 2026-06-12](https://horizon3.ai/attack-research/disclosures/cve-2026-48558-simplehelp-authentication-bypass-iocs/)); on managed endpoints, `node.exe` executing a `jquery.js` from an unexpected path after a remote-support session ([Blackpoint Cyber, 2026-06-29](https://blackpointcyber.com/blog/a-djinn-in-the-machine-taskweavers-node-js-intrusion-chain/)).

**Defender takeaway:** on any SimpleHelp server that ran an affected build with OIDC group logins, review the technician list and the sessions they opened before relying on the upgrade, since a planted technician survives it.
